<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blue Division</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as División Azul, 250th Infantry Division</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blue Division was a unit of Spanish volunteers, mostly Falangists and army men, that fought for Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the Eastern Front as the 250th Infantry Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>. Sent by Franco in 1941 after the German invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, some 45,000 men served in it, chiefly on the Leningrad front. Under Allied pressure it was withdrawn in late 1943, though a smaller Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> stayed until early 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blue-division.jpg" alt="Blue Division" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotograf%C3%ADa_de_la_bandera_espa%C3%B1ola_presidiendo_un_acto_de_la_Divisi%C3%B3n_Azul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
